{"authored" [{"question" "q-162685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}\\,i+t\\,j+\\sqrt{t-2}\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/state.json" "file_sha256" "3904bf5aa136c2350759b4bbf662ea0b27bc705c580f0d92b5bd5d2096856886" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty -2)\\cup (-2,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{t-2}$ is $t\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/18bd3e410f2f3e415e8164ae6e2f9e03a2f7281ce15e6e05cb570ecfdac10964.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/state.json" "file_sha256" "3904bf5aa136c2350759b4bbf662ea0b27bc705c580f0d92b5bd5d2096856886" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable