n-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle \\sqrt{t+3}, \\: t^3-t^2, \\: \\dfrac{1}{t+1}\\right\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/example-12350.json" "file_sha256" "3b5762e366b9fbdf21c6dfec4bacb6ef68d7da435b0dd835176902f48e87fa8a" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{t+3}$ is $t \\in [-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3-t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\dfrac{1}{t+1}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/9577ea515e58e4db49ee5a5ef9f923bc8b34e2dc232b904c956cc1b778141f14.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in [-3,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/example-12350.json" "file_sha256" "3b5762e366b9fbdf21c6dfec4bacb6ef68d7da435b0dd835176902f48e87fa8a" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = \\ln{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{1}{t-2} \\, \\mathbf{j} + e^{2t} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/example-12362.json" "file_sha256" "fd9aa95c59a84545ebcdf9b9dac63c0eb094f49552c29776b5152b8c2a4dbe73" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\ln{t}$ is $t \\in (0, \\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\dfrac{1}{t-2}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{2t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/2a4931e8dc80192219736fc4dbc586ad2405bbb628d75bd0e17fa9616567d793.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/example-12362.json" "file_sha256" "fd9aa95c59a84545ebcdf9b9dac63c0eb094f49552c29776b5152b8c2a4dbe73" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f (t) = \\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right) \\, \\mathbf i + t^3 \\,\\mathbf j + e^t \\,\\mathbf k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[5pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/example-12455.json" "file_sha256" "f489cdc93302e671abfeb4aa382700b87cc0d540f72c6aeb34eb61f5da4901c2" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nt =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/example-12455.json" "file_sha256" "f489cdc93302e671abfeb4aa382700b87cc0d540f72c6aeb34eb61f5da4901c2" "basis" 1239 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-160771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\sqrt{2+t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3+t}\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e078e637b0a5016d70c6690103f14d012c3146cab810198ec878e6e2ed5d82f"} {"question" "q-160771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{2+t}$ is $t\\in [-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\frac{1}{3+t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $1+{t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/assets/01998bcc54d297275af928b2467e7bcc077c84fd595567f5b3ff5bf89d38ec7b.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in [-2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e078e637b0a5016d70c6690103f14d012c3146cab810198ec878e6e2ed5d82f"} {"question" "q-160771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c7e228c7c5c3c966d12a7927a28225e9bc1ff50e1eeb272522eaf8cbee331d1"} {"question" "q-160771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e078e637b0a5016d70c6690103f14d012c3146cab810198ec878e6e2ed5d82f"} {"question" "q-160771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\in (-2,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty )"] ["math" "t\\in (-2,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )"] ["math"