eloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e078e637b0a5016d70c6690103f14d012c3146cab810198ec878e6e2ed5d82f"} {"question" "e-12350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle \\sqrt{t+3}, \\: t^3-t^2, \\: \\dfrac{1}{t+1}\\right\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/example-12350.json" "file_sha256" "42f0bdcb5bea0626175e4853b293d08345557c083e4a1cc42bac46d28a9912e9"} {"question" "e-12350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{t+3}$ is $t \\in [-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3-t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\dfrac{1}{t+1}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/assets/9577ea515e58e4db49ee5a5ef9f923bc8b34e2dc232b904c956cc1b778141f14.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in [-3,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/example-12350.json" "file_sha256" "42f0bdcb5bea0626175e4853b293d08345557c083e4a1cc42bac46d28a9912e9"} {"question" "e-12362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = \\ln{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{1}{t-2} \\, \\mathbf{j} + e^{2t} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/example-12362.json" "file_sha256" "e2fdfd7d9b41db079c30a0acd071a9a711c392df001684a44d0e459869caf95b"} {"question" "e-12362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\ln{t}$ is $t \\in (0, \\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\dfrac{1}{t-2}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{2t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/assets/2a4931e8dc80192219736fc4dbc586ad2405bbb628d75bd0e17fa9616567d793.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/example-12362.json" "file_sha256" "e2fdfd7d9b41db079c30a0acd071a9a711c392df001684a44d0e459869caf95b"} {"question" "e-12455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f (t) = \\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right) \\, \\mathbf i + t^3 \\,\\mathbf j + e^t \\,\\mathbf k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[5pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/example-12455.json" "file_sha256" "3e52f16dcf3535d8b873bede7e73d48a1398a8021117acef5816e9c95c50bc8b"} {"question" "e-12455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nt =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/example-12455.json" "file_sha256" "3e52f16dcf3535d8b873bede7e73d48a1398a8021117acef5816e9c95c50bc8b"}] "usage" {"q-162685" [] "q-162646" [] "e-12350" [] "q-160773" [] "q-162635" [] "q-162652" [] "e-12362" [] "q-160771" [] "e-12455" []} "basis" 2786 "retractions" [[17592186115428 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t) = \\sqrt{2 + t}i + \\frac{1}{3 + t}j + (1 + t^{2})k$."] [17592186294940 :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty -2)\\cup (-2,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{t-2}$ is $t\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/18bd3e410f2f3e415e8164ae6e2f9e03a2f7281ce15e6e05cb570ecfdac10964.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty ).$"] [17592186115429 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t) = \\sqrt{4 - t^{2}}i + \\ln (t - 1)j + e^{t}k$."] [17592186294912 :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the 